ts, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nBudget line is standard compliance practice, doesn't change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations, just makes cost visible. That's paradigm preserving? Maybe slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new element (budget line) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. Hmm budget visibility might be slightly new elements. The description for 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Budget line is a process/format. Might be 2. Let's decide 1? It says standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Ring-fencing budget does change way of working financially. Probably paradigm preserving? I'd argue it's paradigm preserving: it's just accounting, not changing R&D/compliance relationship.\n\nMaybe 1. I'll pick 1.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nRing-fencing budget line for compliance is common, mundane. So originality=1.\n\nRelevance applicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem: lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing resources. Budget line addresses meeting mandate by funding it. So applicability? It applies directly. Does it solve identified problem? Problem is how to meet logging mandate. Funding allocation helps. Might be 4? Or 3 solves implied problem. The stated problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or