mplementation is common. Let's give 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is building prototype to de-risk logging architecture. It introduces tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That is level 2. Not changing relationship between R&D and compliance fundamentally, just internal benchmark.\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nReference build with annotated codebase for fork. That's fairly common engineering practice. Maybe interesting but not rare. I'd say 2 = interesting? Or 1 common mundane? Might be common. I'd lean 2 = interesting. Could be 3 unusual? Probably not. Let's choose 2.\n\nApplicability: 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem)\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 intervention not stated...\n\nProposal addresses logging mandate for biometric system specifically Article 12(3). Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Building reference build directly addresses meeting logging mandate by providing pattern. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness: 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 unreasonable or unlikely\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill a single prototype benchmark solve the lab's logging mandate? It de-risks unknowns before committing all teams. It contributes but does not fully solve implementation across all projects. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial. Could argue if you could do it, it would solve main problem? No, still need teams to adopt. So 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... Might be list of key-values. Provide lines.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P1020\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nImplementability evidence: Assigning one senior engineer for six weeks on existing infrastructure is a reasonably feasible measure with limited scope. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building an internal reference prototype and sharing annotated code internally is a common, legally compliant engineering practice that violates no norms. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces a benchmark tool and process for logging but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing how R&D relates to compliance. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Creating an annotated reference implementation is an interesting but fairly standard engineering de-risking approach. Score 2.\n\nApplicability evidence: The prototype directly targets the Article 12(3) logging requirements for Annex III biometric systems that the lab must meet. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: A single benchmark will help teams adopt compliant logging patterns but does not by itself implement logging across all high-risk systems. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
