sed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nMandatory spec gate is somewhat common in regulated industries. Traceability spec as kickoff gate is fairly standard compliance practice. Not particularly ingenious. Might be common/mundane? Could be interesting. Probably 2 = interesting. Or 1 = common. Hmm. Many labs have design gates. Might be common. I'd say 2 interesting but not unusual. Let's pick 2.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore.\n\nSpec gate ensures logging considered early, mapping events to Article12. Will solve? It ensures traceability spec exists but does not guarantee logs technically implemented correctly. It contributes to solution, partial. Probably 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. Could be 4? Without regard for workability, would it solve? Might ensure design intent but still need implementation. I'd say 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nThis directly addresses logging mandate by making spec gate. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially/legal/politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon but does not offend\nThis is standard governance, acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = signi