        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P1815: Triage Pipeline Systems by Retrofit Cost. One-quarter audit classifying each in-pipeline system as log-ready, needing middleware patch, or requiring full architectural rework against Article 12 minimum fields. Each group lead runs assessment against Recital 71 criteria; findings feed directly into next-year budget allocation to fund patches before they become release blockers. Targeted spending on few systems that truly need redesign preserves funding for core scientific ambitions rather than spreading uniform compliance tax across every project.\n\nRate subscales:\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab subject to regulation must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal is triage by retrofit cost to meet Article 12 minimum fields. That directly applies. Solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). So applicability =4.\n\nEvidence sentence: The audit classifies in-pipeline systems against Article 12 minimum logging fields and feeds budget allocation to meet the mandate.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will contribute? It helps prioritize but does it solve logging mandate? Triage ensures resources targeted, patches before release blockers. Will it solve stated problem? Probably contributes to solution, not fully solves logging for all systems; it's partial. Level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? \"will solve the stated problem without regard for workability\". If you could do it, would it solve main problem? Triage alone doesn't create logs, just allocates budget. So partial. So effectiveness 3.\n\nEvidence: Triage prioritizes spending but does not itself build logging capabilities, only directs resources toward them.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nThis is triage audit for compliance cost. That's standard compliance practice, maybe slightly new element of cost-based triage but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2? Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a cost-based audit process but keeps compliance framed as a logging retrofit rather than reshaping R&D-compliance relationship.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 interesting 1 common mundane boring\n\nTriage by retrofit cost is common project management. Common, mundane. So originality 1.\n\nEvidence: Cost-based triage audits for regulatory retrofits are a common, mundane resource-allocation practice in R&D labs.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nOne-quarter audit classifying systems... group leads run assessment. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Maybe requires audit effort. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: A one-quarter audit led by existing group leads is a reasonably feasible organizational change with modest coordination cost.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 radically violates\n\nThis is acceptable. Common strategy. 4.\n\nEvidence: Prioritizing compliance spending by retrofit cost aligns with normal budget stewardship and violates no legal or business norms.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (an integer). Likely output as list of lines or JSON object.\n\nI'll produce JSON with id and fields.\n\nMake sure order: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\napplicability_evidence: \"The audit directly classifies in-pipeline systems against Article 12 minimum logging fields and links findings to budget for compliance.\"\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: \"Triage directs scarce resources to the highest-need systems but does not itself create the required logs or guarantee full compliance.\"\neffectiveness: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"It introduces a cost-based triage process yet still treats compliance as a logging retrofit rather than changing the R&D-compliance relationship.\"\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\noriginality_evidence: \"Cost-based triage audits for regulatory retrofits are a common, mundane resource allocation practice in R&D labs.\"\noriginality: 1\n\nimplementability_evidence: \"A one-quarter audit run by existing group leads requires modest coordination and is reasonably feasible to implement.\"\nimplementability: 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: \"Prioritizing compliance spending by retrofit cost is standard budget stewardship that violates no legal, social or business norms.\"\nacceptability: 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
