tion\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nOpen benchmark for compliance-aware system evaluation is unusual. Probably originality 3? Could be 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is open source benchmark, release paper. Acceptable. Likely 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nBuild benchmark atop internal logging infrastructure, release with paper, maintain versioned updates tracking amendments... That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be significant change, expensive or difficult? Building open source benchmark is non-radical but requires resources. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Possibly 3.\n\nIf build atop internal infrastructure, might be easy? Maintaining versioned updates tracking amendments across Member States is ongoing cost. So 3.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably need JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text lines.\n\nI'll output as list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1545\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
