ip between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what the lab researches. So yes qualifies.\n\nBut also \"paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\". Hmm both change relationship. Difference is breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship. Stretching just changes relationship. The proposal introduces new elements (structured publishable artifacts). So maybe 4.\n\nLet's pick paradigm_relatedness = 4? Might be 3. Let's think: Is it paradigm breaking? Changing compliance to open science is significant but still within compliance obligation. Could be considered stretching not breaking. The anchor says paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. That fits exactly. So 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2=interesting. 1=common mundane.\n\nOpen technical documentation as field-level knowledge product is unusual, interesting, shows some imagination. Is it not expressed before? Possibly similar ideas exist. I'd say unusual interesting. So 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not impossible. 1 = totally infeasible.\n\nRecasting documentation into publishable artifacts, partnering with compliance, submitting to journals requires effort, IP review, anonymization, peer-review rigour. Some changes, reasonably feasible? Might be significant change, expensive. Probably needs resources, IP legal review. Could be some changes, reasonably feasible. I'd say 3. Maybe 2 because requires building documentation standard matching peer-review rigour; that's significant. But labs can do it. I'll go with 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. 2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable. 1 = radically violates laws...\n\nPublishing anonymised documentation is acceptable, maybe unusual but not offensive. 3? Or 4? Common strategies that violate no norms: open science is common. Might be 4. However releasing technical documentation may conflict with IP/confidentiality, but proposal says where IP permits. So acceptable. Probably 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill publishing documentation solve logging mandate? It addresses technical documentation mandated by Recital 71, but the problem is about logging mandate per Article 12 record-keeping (automatic recording of events logs). The proposal focuses on technical documentation, not logs. It recasts technical documentation into publishable artifacts. Does that meet logging mandate? Logging mandate is automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, specific logging capabilities. This proposal is about documentation, not logs. So effectiveness is partial? Might contribute to compliance culture but does not solve logging requirement. The stated problem: how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal adapts R&D by making documentation open science, which may help compliance but doesn't directly provide logs. Effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Or 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nIt addresses technical documentation requirement from Recital 71, not Article 12 logging. The proposal mentions technical documentation mandated by Recital 71 — algorithms, data provenance... Not logs. So it does not meet logging mandate. Could be unrelated? It is related to compliance obligation generally. Effectiveness maybe 3 (contribute). Applicability?\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? The problem is meeting logging mandate. Proposal addresses technical documentation, not logs. So maybe solves implied problem related to compliance. Could be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y...). Or 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates.\n\nI think applicability is 3: it applies to compliance obligations generally, which is related to logging mandate, but not directly logs. Could be 2? Let's decide: The proposal explicitly references Recital 71 technical documentation, not Article 12 logs. So it's about compliance but different requirement. Still part of same regulation. Might be considered directly related.\n\nThe instrument says applicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Stated problem includes meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Adapting R&D accordingly could include making documentation open science. So maybe applies.\n\nI'll give applicability = 3? Hmm.\n\nAlternative interpretation: Effectiveness = 2? Because it probably will not work to solve logging problem. It solves documentation, not logs. Might be unreasonable.\n\nLet's parse: Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal adapts R&D by publishing documentation. That is adaptation. So effectiveness could be 3 (contributes). Applicability = 3 or 4.\n\nGiven evidence requirement one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal reframes mandated technical documentation as publishable open-science outputs, thereby changing the relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Score 4.\n\noriginality_evidence: Turning regulatory documentation into peer-reviewed knowledge artifacts is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine compliance filing. Score 3.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building peer-review-level documentation standards and submitting anonymised case studies requires legal review, staff time and coordination but is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Open-science sharing of anonymised methods is socially acceptable in research and does not violate norms when IP permits. Score 4? Hmm earlier said 3. Maybe 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Open science is common. I'll go 4.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: It does not create automatic event logs required by Article 12 but strengthens documentation culture and reputational incentives that support overall compliance adaptation. Score 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses the Recital 71 documentation obligation tied to the same high-risk AI compliance problem, even though it targets documentation rather than logging. Score 3.\n\nCheck ordering.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nOutput format? Probably JSON with fields. Instruction: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Provide rating. Likely list.\n\nWill produce:\n\nid: P1345\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 4\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence each evidence.\n\nMake sure sentences end with period.\n\nProceed.",
