reaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Does it address larger problem of what lab researches? Maybe not breaking. I think 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nCreating templated suite co-authored by researchers at each milestone... That requires process change, researcher time. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is publishing documentation. Acceptable. No violation. 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore.\nProposal focuses on documentation not logging? Recital 71 mentions documentation and logs. Article 12 is record-keeping / logging. Proposal talks about documentation as reproducibility infrastructure, satisfies Annex III 1(a) fields. It does not address automatic recording of events/logs per Art12(1). It addresses documentation requirement from Recital 71. Problem states must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Does this solve logging mandate? It partially addresses record-keeping via documentation, but logging capabilities for events is core. So it contributes but partial. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Could be 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\nThe proposal addresses documentation part of compliance, not logging events. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. Documentation is related but not the logging mandate per Article 12. Might be implied? It says record-keeping includes logs. Documentation is separate. Could be applicability 3? Or 4? The problem mentions meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Adapting R&D to treat documentation as reproducibility is adaptation. Might be directly related. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal text: \"Treat the Recital 71 requirement for clear documentation... not as regulatory checkbox but as our lab's reproducibility standard...\" That's about documentation, not logging. Article 12 is record-keeping logs. So applicability may be 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Might still apply.\n\nLet's choose applicability 3.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence sentences then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ...\n\nWe can output simple list.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P1311\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The proposal reframes mandatory compliance documentation as publishable reproducibility infrastructure, which is unusual in AI labs and shows imagination beyond checkbox compliance. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It changes the relationship between research work and compliance by weaving documentation into scientific publication and grant merit, rather than treating it as separate logging practice. Score 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating templated documentation co-authored at milestones requires process change and researcher time but uses existing publishing workflows and is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Publishing rigorous methods documentation aligns with academic norms and regulatory transparency 