rch survives scrutiny intact. That suggests changes relationship maybe? Could be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Hmm.\n\nIt is methodological study to map existing practices onto demands. That's introducing new elements but still compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nMapping lab docs to regulatory requirements is common? Might be interesting but not rare. Methodological study interviewing researchers etc is fairly standard compliance mapping. Probably 2 = interesting.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. Proposal is a study mapping documentation, not actual implementation of logging. Will it solve problem? It contributes to understanding gap but does not implement logging. So partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\nProposal applies directly: mapping documentation to Article12 traceability demands is directly about meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Yes mapping helps meet mandate. Possibly 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is study interviews audit co-design white paper. Acceptable. Likely 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult...\nStudy over one academic year interviewing 12 researchers, audit five prototypes, co-design iteratively. That's resource intensive but feasible. Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. Implementability 3? Could be 3.\n\nCould argue implementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Yes.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProvide output.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP1281\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
