        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2465.\n\nText: Investigating Lifetime Drift Under Continuous Logging. Launch dedicated research track studying how high-risk AI systems' decision boundaries... evolve over months continuous operation when full event logs retained per Article 12(1). Instrument prototypes with paragraph-3 minimum logging set from day one, then apply statistical process-control methods to detect drift patterns that post-market monitoring under Article 72 would flag. This positions lab at genuine knowledge frontier...\n\nWe need six subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is to launch research track studying drift under continuous logging. Does it solve problem of meeting logging mandate? It instruments prototypes with minimum logging set from day one, applies SPC to detect drift. That helps meet logging mandate and adapt R&D. Effectiveness: will contribute to solution? It helps comply via logging implementation and provides insights for post-market monitoring. Is it reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably contributes but not fully solves mandate? Mandate is to meet logging and adapt R&D. Research track does adapt R&D and implements logging. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Might be 4? \"reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability\". Does it solve main problem? Main problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Launching research track with instrumentation does meet logging for prototypes. But does it solve compliance for all systems? Partial. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Proposal does implement logging per Article 12 and adapt R&D. Seems directly related. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Launch dedicated research track, instrument prototypes with paragraph-3 minimum logging set from day one, apply SPC methods. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Research track may be moderate cost. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2 = significant change expensive/difficult. Hmm. Implementing continuous logging for Annex III 1(a) prototypes with full logs is required anyway. Adding statistical process control research is extra. Probably reasonably feasible. I'll say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Research on drift is acceptable. No violation. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Maybe somewhat uncommon but not offend. I'd say 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal weaves logging into scientific research about drift, changes relationship? It positions lab at knowledge frontier. Might be paradigm stretching: logging woven into science. That is 3. Could be 2? Introduces new elements (tools) but still treats compliance as logging practice? It actually uses compliance logs as research data. That's changing relationship. I'd say 3 = paradigm stretching.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nInvestigating lifetime drift under continuous logging is unusual interesting. Not completely novel? Might be 3. Could be 2? I'd say 3.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: probably list.\n\nProvide each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce.\n\n",
