ing scientific frontier. That is changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: logging woven into science. That's paradigm stretching? Could be 3. Paradigm breaking would require changes larger problem of what lab researches. It does address long-horizon behavioral change, maybe still within AI research. I'd say 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into the science). Maybe even 4? \"introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between the lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses the larger problem of what the lab researches\". It does both introduce new elements and change relationship. Might be 4. Let's choose 3 to be safe? The description for 4: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Actually \"or\". So changing relationship qualifies for 4. Hmm 4 says paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship... or addresses larger problem. The phrasing ambiguous. Probably 3 is stretching, 4 is breaking. Does repurposing logs break paradigm? Might be stretching.\n\nI'll pick paradigm_relatedness = 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nWill this solve problem? It meets logging mandate (emit compliance-grade logs) and adapts R&D. So reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. It would solve both compliance and adaptation. Might be partial? It solves logging mandate and uses logs for research. Seems full solution. I'd say 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Logging mandate + adapt R&D. Yes. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Repurposing logs requires defining hypotheses, instrument pipelines to emit compliance-grade logs on standard schema, run quarterly analyses. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Might need engineering. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Using compliance logs for research - may raise privacy concerns? Logs include input data for biometric matches per Art 12(3)(c). Using for research could be problematic with GDPR. But proposal says compliance-grade logs on standard schema. Might be acceptable if anonymized. Probably somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Could be 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Or 4? Common strategies? Repurposing logs for research is not common? Might be acceptable. I'll give 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give... Could be plain lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2827\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nOriginality evidence: The proposal frames mandatory Article 12 logs as a novel longitudinal research instrument rather than a cost center, which is unusual among expected compliance responses.\nScore 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It weaves compliance logging into the scientific workflow by making log streams the ex