 logging practice\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is design logging for Annex III systems, map pipeline to mandated log fields, implement structured logging. This is standard compliance logging practice; lab's way unchanged? It restructures systems to natively capture. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance. I think it's standard compliance and logging practice. The proposal is basically do what Article 12 requires. That's paradigm preserving. Might be 1.\n\nBut text says \"restructures our biometric identification systems to natively capture...\" That could be slightly stretching. Hmm.\n\nThe instrument expects: standard compliance = paradigm preserving. I think 1.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious.\nCommon, mundane, boring =1. Unusual interesting shows some imagination=3. This is common compliance engineering. So 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related => 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProposal does logging for Annex III systems mandated by Article 12(3). Directly addresses mandate. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =>4\nIf you could do it, it would solve main problem: meeting logging mandate for biometric systems. Yes. So 4.\n\nProvide output format: rating of proposal echoing id. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... give ... first and then ... Might be plain text lines. Safer to output structured.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2449\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The proposal implements mandated logging with encryption and access controls, conforming to legal requirements and data protection norms. -> score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: It requires a dedicated product-level engineering workstream to map pipelines and add structured logging, which is reasonably feasible but not trivially low cost. -> score 3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The work treats compliance as standard logging practice without altering the lab's R&D paradigm or its relationship to compliance obligations. -> score 1\n\nOriginality evidence: Building mandated biometric logs into verification workflows is a common, routine compliance engineering response. -> score 1\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses how to meet Article 12(1)-(3) logging requirements for Annex III biometric systems. -> score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, native capture of the four minimum log fields would satisfy the Act's logging mandate and avoid post-launch enforcement risk. -> score 4\n\nCheck anchored levels: acceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities - yes.\n\nImplementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures - ok.\n\nParadigm_relatedness 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance... lab way unchanged - ok.\n\nOriginality 1 = common mundane boring - ok.\n\nApplicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related - ok.\n\nEffectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability - ok.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
