andate. This addresses biometric subset. Could be partial solution. Effectiveness definition: will solve stated problem without regard workability. It would solve logging for those systems, but maybe not all high-risk systems. The problem is general. Might be partial.\n\nThe proposal focuses on Annex III biometric systems. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate generally. This solves for that subset. Is it full solution? Could be partial.\n\nEffectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial solution). Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: It ensures compliance for the strictest logging floor systems but leaves other high-risk AI systems unaddressed.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost non-radical; 3 some changes reasonably feasible; 2 significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible; 1 totally infeasible\n\nDesign product-level engineering workstream to restructure biometric identification systems to natively capture four data points with encryption/access controls. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Restructuring pipelines and adding structured logging with encryption requires engineering effort and coordination but uses standard logging practices.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 common strategies violate no norms\nThis is compliance with law, encryption for personal data. Common. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building mandated logs with encryption and access controls aligns with legal requirements and data protection norms.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common mundane boring\n\nThis is basically standard compliance engineering. Probably common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Mapping pipeline stages to mandated log fields is a routine compliance implementation expected by experienced evaluators.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is product-level engineering workstream to natively capture logs. That's standard compliance/logging practice. Possibly slightly paradigm stretching? It restructures systems to natively capture. Might be standard.\n\nProbably paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: The workstream treats compliance as an add-on logging requirement without altering R&D goals or methods.\n\nNeed output format. Echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Order listed: applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide JSON? Probably just lines. Might be structured.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give ... first and then ...\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2449\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's produce concise sentences.\n\n",
