m day one is non-radical? It requires organizational change. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Creating a dedicated logging architect role and embedding schema design in CI/CD requires staffing and process changes but uses existing development practices.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Logging for compliance is common. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Building compliance logging into engineering is standard practice and aligns with legal obligations without offending sensibilities.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious. Proposal is common, mundane? Embed logging early is best practice. Might be common mundane boring =1. Or interesting? The description \"logging architect\" maybe unusual. But generally building in logging from start is common advice. I'd rate 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Embedding logs early and testing them in CI/CD is a widely known software engineering practice for regulated systems.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged? Actually introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice -> slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice =2? Or paradigm preserving =1 standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. The proposal changes process to build in from start, introduces logging architect. That is slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Definition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nProposal does change re